WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:06.850
So with just days left in their session before
the holiday break, that is upcoming, Democrats

00:00:06.850 --> 00:00:13.650
and Republicans in Congress are reportedly
getting closer to a second coronavirus stimulus

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:20.430
package, a relief bill, as they're often called,
finally at the end of December.

00:00:20.430 --> 00:00:24.210
They are closer, but nothing is yet guaranteed.

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:30.440
And I don't actually know what's worse, that
it's taken seven months to get close to a

00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:36.220
second deal or what would actually be in this
deal, which we found out about over the last

00:00:36.220 --> 00:00:43.490
24 to 48 hours, because this is a deal that
will include ungodly amounts of money for

00:00:43.490 --> 00:00:45.350
many industries.

00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:53.160
And it might include it might include 600
dollar direct payments for some Americans.

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:56.960
Now the information is changing regularly.

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:02.620
One estimate was it might be 900 dollars for
some Americans, 600 is the number that AOC

00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:04.750
and others are talking about.

00:01:04.750 --> 00:01:12.040
But the last estimate is it will be a 900
billion dollar total package with the possibility

00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:16.120
of 600 dollars in direct payments to Americans.

00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:22.640
And in the meantime, it would include that
blanket liability shield for companies against

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:24.720
coronavirus related lawsuits.

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:28.851
That's something Donald Trump has been talking
about after the first stimulus package, when

00:01:28.851 --> 00:01:31.200
discussion of the second one started.

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:35.090
Donald Trump at every opportunity would say,
we want the liability shield for companies

00:01:35.090 --> 00:01:37.400
and they understand what this is.

00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:44.490
A lie, a coronaviruses liability shield for
companies means that if there is an employee

00:01:44.490 --> 00:01:52.290
or a customer that gets the virus because
of how your business handled social distancing

00:01:52.290 --> 00:01:58.140
and and sanitary guidelines, et cetera, you
have blanket protection from being sued for

00:01:58.140 --> 00:01:59.140
that.

00:01:59.140 --> 00:02:05.080
And it is essentially just an open door to
companies to do whatever you can to increase

00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:10.830
profitability in the short run with no regard
whatsoever to the danger that you may be placing

00:02:10.830 --> 00:02:15.060
your employees or customers or both in as
a result of ignoring guidelines which may

00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:16.660
make you more profitable.

00:02:16.660 --> 00:02:18.480
It's that that would be in there.

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:22.020
But they're battling for 600 dollar payments
for individuals.

00:02:22.020 --> 00:02:26.209
The package, again, as of the latest iteration
that we've seen reporting on it, would not

00:02:26.209 --> 00:02:31.060
include any assistance to state and local
governments, which is sort of where you need

00:02:31.060 --> 00:02:36.280
to start to properly manage the different
ways in which this is affecting different

00:02:36.280 --> 00:02:37.660
municipalities and states.

00:02:37.660 --> 00:02:42.410
So it's just so pitiful and embarrassing and
pathetic.

00:02:42.410 --> 00:02:43.660
There's really no other way to say know.

00:02:43.660 --> 00:02:46.170
Six hundred dollars.

00:02:46.170 --> 00:02:51.980
Some people received twelve hundred back in
May, plus those six hundred dollars a week

00:02:51.980 --> 00:02:55.890
boosts in unemployment for people who were
laid off.

00:02:55.890 --> 00:02:59.080
It's been seven months since that with nothing.

00:02:59.080 --> 00:03:02.300
Who knows even if this is approved.

00:03:02.300 --> 00:03:05.770
And that's a question mark, how quickly the
money would actually be distributed and make

00:03:05.770 --> 00:03:07.780
it into people's bank accounts.

00:03:07.780 --> 00:03:10.930
And they're talking about maybe six hundred
dollars.

00:03:10.930 --> 00:03:15.960
Democrats proposed doing another twelve hundred
and they've been proposing that since May.

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:17.690
That went nowhere.

00:03:17.690 --> 00:03:22.780
Some of the more progressive Democrats, including
friend of the show who we spoke to not that

00:03:22.780 --> 00:03:27.170
long ago, Congressman Roxana, have proposed
two thousand dollars.

00:03:27.170 --> 00:03:29.730
Some have proposed two thousand dollars per
month.

00:03:29.730 --> 00:03:32.280
That's all going nowhere very, very quickly.

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:38.370
And the reality is, you know, I never want
to scoff at anything when we know that half

00:03:38.370 --> 00:03:42.170
the country can't meet an unexpected 400 dollar
expense.

00:03:42.170 --> 00:03:46.330
You see the number six hundred and you say,
well, that's we shouldn't scoff at that.

00:03:46.330 --> 00:03:52.940
OK, but the reality is that 600 dollars, if
you're doing fine, doesn't change really anything.

00:03:52.940 --> 00:03:58.300
And if you're if you've not been doing fine
since May, then what six hundred dollars going

00:03:58.300 --> 00:03:59.580
to do materially.

00:03:59.580 --> 00:04:00.580
I get it.

00:04:00.580 --> 00:04:04.250
You know, maybe this makes it so you can make
half your rent payment next month and that's

00:04:04.250 --> 00:04:05.390
better than nothing.

00:04:05.390 --> 00:04:08.610
But it's not about beggars can't be choosers.

00:04:08.610 --> 00:04:14.099
It's that the argument over whether we give
600 or 900 dollars to people after seven months

00:04:14.099 --> 00:04:19.209
of nothing, given the economic situation,
communicates a complete lack of understanding

00:04:19.209 --> 00:04:23.590
as to what's actually going on in the United
States for tens of millions of Americans right

00:04:23.590 --> 00:04:29.860
now, even the first stimulus with the twelve
hundred dollars, it's fine.

00:04:29.860 --> 00:04:31.949
And it was useful for people.

00:04:31.949 --> 00:04:37.900
But if you were employed at that time and
you were continuing to work to earn a living,

00:04:37.900 --> 00:04:43.050
the twelve hundred dollars was not likely
to affect your viability financially over

00:04:43.050 --> 00:04:44.229
the course of the entire pandemic.

00:04:44.229 --> 00:04:49.589
And that's why stopping short would that and
then going seven months with nothing is delusional.

00:04:49.589 --> 00:04:54.650
What really was the life saver for a lot of
people back in April May was the enhanced

00:04:54.650 --> 00:04:58.780
unemployment of an extra six hundred dollars
a week, which, by the way, in large cities

00:04:58.780 --> 00:04:59.780
particularly.

00:04:59.780 --> 00:05:05.360
If you have even just one child, it's barely
enough to get by without working, and in some

00:05:05.360 --> 00:05:10.150
cities it's not enough to get by and look
at how many businesses were closed at that

00:05:10.150 --> 00:05:11.150
time.

00:05:11.150 --> 00:05:16.680
So this is a great example of how being cheap
is really expensive.

00:05:16.680 --> 00:05:17.780
And we've talked before.

00:05:17.780 --> 00:05:20.199
There's there was an interesting article I
recommended.

00:05:20.199 --> 00:05:26.250
It must have been a year ago, maybe 10 months
ago, about how being poor is really expensive.

00:05:26.250 --> 00:05:33.439
And the article explained, you know, if you're
poor, you're likely going you're more likely

00:05:33.439 --> 00:05:40.249
to go to the bodega on the corner to get four
rolls of toilet paper rather than ordering

00:05:40.249 --> 00:05:47.160
the 60 rolls from Amazon, which are better
quality and cheaper on a per roll basis.

00:05:47.160 --> 00:05:51.340
But you've got to lay out some larger amount
of money upfront.

00:05:51.340 --> 00:05:55.529
And in that sense, it's much more expensive
to be poor because you're often buying items

00:05:55.529 --> 00:06:00.499
at a higher unit cost, sometimes of lower
quality and spending more time acquiring those

00:06:00.499 --> 00:06:02.759
items because you're making multiple trips
instead.

00:06:02.759 --> 00:06:06.530
If you go on Amazon and you get the big box
of toilet paper, it's an example.

00:06:06.530 --> 00:06:11.499
The way it applies here is it actually applies
to a lot of consumer products.

00:06:11.499 --> 00:06:16.129
You buy the really cheap work boots this year
and you probably have to buy another pair

00:06:16.129 --> 00:06:20.710
next year and another pair next year or you
get and that gets really expensive in the

00:06:20.710 --> 00:06:22.080
long run.

00:06:22.080 --> 00:06:25.240
Or you buy the really good boots with the
lifetime warranty.

00:06:25.240 --> 00:06:28.930
You do have to spend more now, but your long
term cost is lower.

00:06:28.930 --> 00:06:34.539
The way it applies to stimulus is you do twelve
hundred bucks in May and maybe 600 now.

00:06:34.539 --> 00:06:38.439
And it sounds like you're saving a lot of
money relative to giving people 1500 dollars

00:06:38.439 --> 00:06:40.669
a month, two thousand dollars a month.

00:06:40.669 --> 00:06:45.020
Sounds like you're saving money, but when
you consider that the cash payments are demand

00:06:45.020 --> 00:06:50.349
side stimulus and they stimulate the economy,
plus higher long term payments allow people

00:06:50.349 --> 00:06:54.020
to stay home and be more responsible, which
slows the spread.

00:06:54.020 --> 00:06:58.300
And we know that every person who ends up
in the hospital for Carone with coronavirus

00:06:58.300 --> 00:07:00.490
places a huge financial cost on the system.

00:07:00.490 --> 00:07:06.379
You realize the higher outlay is cheaper in
the long term by suppressing the spread.

00:07:06.379 --> 00:07:10.089
And case in point, you look at European countries
that are only in their second spike.

00:07:10.089 --> 00:07:15.770
Now, that entire summer period in the northern
hemisphere where they had relatively normal

00:07:15.770 --> 00:07:20.119
summers economically, was much better than
what we've had here where we are now on our

00:07:20.119 --> 00:07:25.009
third peak, we had peak number two during
the summer and it was disastrous for business

00:07:25.009 --> 00:07:27.849
and the economy as we continued to hemorrhage
jobs.

00:07:27.849 --> 00:07:32.710
So that's a long way of saying one of the
richest countries in the world, which some

00:07:32.710 --> 00:07:37.719
love to say is the greatest country in the
world, whatever that means, finds billions

00:07:37.719 --> 00:07:39.939
for corporations in certain industries.

00:07:39.939 --> 00:07:42.569
And we're arguing about six hundred dollars
for people.

00:07:42.569 --> 00:07:43.619
It's pathetic.

00:07:43.619 --> 00:07:46.050
Canada did two grand a month for seven months.

00:07:46.050 --> 00:07:49.509
If you were working, but didn't earn very
much, you qualified, at least for part of

00:07:49.509 --> 00:07:50.509
that.

00:07:50.509 --> 00:07:52.069
Some provinces even did additional payments.

00:07:52.069 --> 00:07:56.719
So what Congresswoman Pelosi and some others
are saying is if you don't find the six hundred

00:07:56.719 --> 00:08:01.629
dollars appropriate, get in touch now with
your elected officials and tell them that.

00:08:01.629 --> 00:08:03.669
But really more is needed.

00:08:03.669 --> 00:08:08.640
I mean, if this doesn't justify mass rallies
at urban centers, I really don't know what

00:08:08.640 --> 00:08:12.349
does don't these members of Congress and senators
work for us?

00:08:12.349 --> 00:08:14.729
And of course, that's true in theory.

00:08:14.729 --> 00:08:16.319
It's true on paper.

00:08:16.319 --> 00:08:19.749
In reality, they're mostly serving the interests
of big corporations.

00:08:19.749 --> 00:08:25.809
How disconnected from reality do you have
to be to think that six hundred dollars after

00:08:25.809 --> 00:08:31.619
seven months of nothing is going to solve
a problem and stimulate the economy?

00:08:31.619 --> 00:08:34.860
It's completely it's magical, delusional thinking

